1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,542
Hiring a freelancer means paying for a promise.

2
00:00:03,542 --> 00:00:08,990
Either the buyer pays first and hopes, or the freelancer works first and hopes.

3
00:00:08,990 --> 00:00:11,911
OutcomeGuard replaces hope with proof.

4
00:00:12,610 --> 00:00:15,935
The payment is authorized on PayPal up front,

5
00:00:15,935 --> 00:00:21,329
but it is captured only when measurable evidence, and the buyer, say yes.

6
00:00:21,329 --> 00:00:25,841
AI drafts, tools verify, humans approve, PayPal executes.

7
00:00:29,043 --> 00:00:31,894
Acme needs a five-hundred-dollar landing page.

8
00:00:31,894 --> 00:00:34,744
The buyer writes the request in plain English,

9
00:00:34,744 --> 00:00:38,215
and Claude turns it into measurable acceptance criteria.

10
00:00:38,215 --> 00:00:40,570
Each one quotes the buyer's own words.

11
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:43,110
Anything that says "must" stays blocking,

12
00:00:43,110 --> 00:00:46,767
and the AI's own idea is only a suggestion, off by default.

13
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:50,485
The buyer adds what only they know, and locks the agreement.

14
00:00:50,485 --> 00:00:55,309
Locking freezes a content hash, so nobody can quietly move the goalposts.

15
00:00:58,523 --> 00:01:00,245
Now the freelancer delivers.

16
00:01:00,245 --> 00:01:05,104
OutcomeGuard opens the page in a real Chromium browser on a phone-sized screen,

17
00:01:05,104 --> 00:01:10,210
submits the contact form to a controlled test endpoint, and runs Google Lighthouse.

18
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:12,486
Three checks pass, in plain language,

19
00:01:12,486 --> 00:01:15,561
with stored screenshots and the Lighthouse report.

20
00:01:15,561 --> 00:01:19,982
Accessibility can't be measured by a tool, so it waits for a human.

21
00:01:20,703 --> 00:01:23,446
Here is the first delivery, recorded earlier.

22
00:01:23,446 --> 00:01:27,286
The form was submitted, but the success message never appeared.

23
00:01:27,286 --> 00:01:30,329
That single failed check blocked the payment.

24
00:01:33,536 --> 00:01:36,068
The accessibility decision is recorded,

25
00:01:36,068 --> 00:01:39,443
and labelled in the app as a scripted demo decision.

26
00:01:39,443 --> 00:01:41,715
The payment policy is re-evaluated:

27
00:01:41,715 --> 00:01:42,299
eligible.

28
00:01:42,299 --> 00:01:44,506
The buyer approves, then captures.

29
00:01:44,506 --> 00:01:46,388
Two separate, explicit steps.

30
00:01:46,388 --> 00:01:50,842
In this recording the payment is simulated, and the app says so.

31
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:55,945
And it isn't only a simulation.

32
00:01:55,945 --> 00:02:01,451
On PayPal Sandbox, we captured a five-hundred-dollar payment through this exact governance,

33
00:02:01,451 --> 00:02:07,260
refunded one hundred after both parties approved, and cancelled an authorization before capture.

34
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:09,677
Every ID was read back from PayPal.

35
00:02:10,403 --> 00:02:11,416
OutcomeGuard.

36
00:02:11,416 --> 00:02:13,675
Don't just pay for a promise.

37
00:02:13,675 --> 00:02:15,065
Pay for proof.
